      "findings": [
        "A1: `## Build plan` is absent. generate_instruction.md 2.1 makes it NOT baseline (6/6 shipped reference briefs omit it, and a numbered build sequence removes the design work this benchmark measures), so the brief carries the other ten canonical H2s plus the optional `## Front-end specification`. QC_instruction A1 predates that decision; the settled rule wins and the deviation is recorded rather than silently taken.",
        "B3: the third difficulty lever, `## Build plan`, is withheld by the same 2.1 rule. `## Technical requirements` and `## Data model` are both present, and `spec_sections_given` records the omission honestly.",
        "C3: dead surface - `PATCH /api/pages/{slug}` appears exactly once, in the API shapes table. It is the endpoint every publish and unpublish rule turns on, so the surface is live in behaviour even though the literal is stated once. Recorded, not repaired, because repeating an endpoint to satisfy a count is noise.",
        "C5: the Data model states the publish-collision invariant as an observable outcome ('two publishes that would collide on one route must not both succeed, exactly one wins and the other is rejected') rather than naming a unique index or a transaction. C5 asks for the storage-level construct and the application-level-checks reminder; INV9, G43 (`prescription_lint.py`) and the variant-b rule in generate_instruction.md 4 forbid both. The newer rule wins.",
        "C7: `## Core features` (18,250 chars), `## User flow` and `## UI/UX notes` sit past run_rubric's 2,500-char slice, and the join sits past 9,000. generate_instruction.md 4 settles this: the brief has no length limit, `window_lint.py` reports past-slice as information and fails nothing, and cutting a real rule to fit trades reward-driving completeness for a diagnostic number. The companion is 1,883 lines and carrying it is the point of the task."
      ]
    },
